<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Algernon Parsons</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1854">1854</a>–<a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Charles Algernon Parsons</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Charles Algernon Parsons was an English mechanical engineer and inventor, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1854 as the youngest son of the astronomer William Parsons, 3rd Earl of Rosse, who designed the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-turbine">steam turbine</a> in 1884. At Clarke, Chapman and Co. near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a> he built a turbine turning at 18,000 RPM and used it to drive an electrical generator of his own design. In 1889 he founded C. A. Parsons and Company in Newcastle, and his Turbinia of 1894 was the first steamship powered by a steam turbine, changing marine propulsion. He was knighted in 1911 and died in 1931 on board the Duchess of Richmond.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-algernon-parsons.jpg" alt="Charles Algernon Parsons" width="330" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Algernon_Parsons.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Parsons belonged to an aristocratic Anglo-Irish family whose seat is Birr Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-offaly">County Offaly</a>, Ireland. The town of Birr was called Parsonstown, after the family, from 1620 to 1901. With his three brothers he was educated at home by private tutors, among them John Purser and Sir Robert Ball, who also acted as practical assistants to the Earl in his astronomical work.</p><p>He read mathematics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a> and then at St. John's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, graduating in 1877 with a first-class honours degree. He then joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a> engineering firm of W. G. Armstrong as an apprentice, an unusual step for an earl's son. Later he moved to Kitsons in Leeds, where he worked on rocket-powered torpedoes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At Clarke, Chapman and Co. Parsons became head of electrical-equipment development and used Regnault's collection of steam data to develop his turbine. Another turbine of the time, Gustaf de Laval's, was an impulse design that exposed the machine to great centrifugal forces and limited its output. In his Rede Lecture of 1911 Parsons explained that he had split the fall in steam pressure into small expansions across a large number of turbines in series, so that the steam velocity nowhere became great. In 1890 Forth Banks Power Station, run by his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a> and District Electric Lighting Company, became the first power station in the world to generate electricity with turbo generators.</p><p>On 26 June 1897 the Turbinia appeared unannounced at the fleet review for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>'s Diamond Jubilee at Spithead, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> and the Lords of the Admiralty. It ran at 34 knots, while the fastest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> ships reached 27. Within two years the destroyers HMS Viper and Cobra had Parsons's turbines, followed by the passenger steamer TS King Edward in 1901, the transatlantic liners Victorian and Virginian in 1905, and HMS Dreadnought in 1906.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Parsons was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in 1898 and received its Copley Medal in 1928. He was president of the British Association for the Advancement of Science from 1916 to 1919 and became a member of the Order of Merit in 1927.</p><p>He died on 11 February 1931 on board the steamship Duchess of Richmond, on a cruise with his wife, of neuritis. A memorial service was held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> on 3 March, and he was buried at St Bartholomew's church in Kirkwhelpington, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>. The Parsons turbine company survives in Heaton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a>, as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siemens">Siemens</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles Algernon Parsons” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
